---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Concert Seat Selection Basics: Floor, Lower Bowl & Upper Terrace | StageDrill';
const description =
  "Floor, lower bowl or upper terrace? A plain-English guide to reading an arena map, with StageDrill's fictional Starlight Civic Arena as the worked example.";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/guides/concert-seat-selection-basics/"
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="mb-4 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-500">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-slate-900">Home</a>
      <span class="mx-1.5">/</span>
      <span class="text-slate-900">Concert Seat Selection Basics</span>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="display-h1 text-slate-950">
      Concert Seat Selection Basics: Floor, Lower Bowl &amp; Upper Terrace
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
      Before you can pick good seats fast, you need to know what you are looking at. An arena map is three tiers with different trade-offs, and the right tier depends on what you value: closeness, view, or price. This guide uses StageDrill&rsquo;s fictional Starlight Civic Arena &mdash; five sections, three tiers, virtual prices &mdash; so you can learn the layout without any real money on the line.
    </p>

    <section class="mt-8 space-y-6 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 sm:text-base">
      <h2 class="section-h2 text-slate-950">The three tiers</h2>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-950">Floor &mdash; closest to the stage</h3>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
          <li>
            <strong>FL-A Floor Center West &mdash; $195</strong> and{' '}
            <strong>FL-B Floor Center East &mdash; $185</strong>
          </li>
          <li>
            The floor puts you at stage level, in the middle of the crowd energy. It is the closest you get, and the most expensive tier in the arena.
          </li>
          <li>
            The trade-off is the view: the floor is flat, so a tall row in front of you is a wall. You feel the show more than you see all of it.
          </li>
          <li>
            Center floor (FL-A and FL-B) faces the stage head-on. The West/East split is just which half of the floor you are on.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-950">Lower Bowl &mdash; the usual sweet spot</h3>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
          <li>
            <strong>LOW-101 Lower Bowl West &mdash; $135</strong> and{' '}
            <strong>LOW-102 Lower Bowl East &mdash; $125</strong>
          </li>
          <li>
            The lower bowl is raised and raked, so the row in front of you sits below your eyeline. You can see the whole stage and still make out faces.
          </li>
          <li>
            For most shows this is the hardest tier to regret: close enough to feel present, high enough to see everything.
          </li>
          <li>
            West and East are mirror choices at the same height. If the price is equal, pick the side nearer your entrance.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-950">Upper Terrace &mdash; the full picture, cheapest</h3>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
          <li>
            <strong>UP-201 Upper Terrace Center &mdash; $75</strong>
          </li>
          <li>
            Highest and farthest, and by far the cheapest seat in the arena ($75 against $195 on the floor). You see the entire production &mdash; staging, lights, screens &mdash; at the cost of detail.
          </li>
          <li>
            Best value when the show is a spectacle, when you are bringing kids, or when the budget is the boss.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 sm:text-base">
      <h2 class="section-h2 text-slate-950">How to read a map in ten seconds</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>Find the stage first.</strong> Everything on the map is relative to it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Center beats side at the same height.</strong> A center upper seat often shows you more than a side lower seat.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Height beats closeness when you want the full picture.</strong> If the show has big staging, a raised seat sees what the floor cannot.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Price is the map&rsquo;s honest signal.</strong> In this arena the tiers run $195 / $185 / $135 / $125 / $75, tracking distance from the stage almost exactly. Real sales blur this with dynamic pricing; here it is clean.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Check the aisle before you commit.</strong> In this arena each row splits into a left block (Seats 1&ndash;4) and a right block (Seats 5&ndash;8), and Seats 4 and 5 are not adjacent. The full rule is in{' '}
          <a href="/guides/seat-selection-practice/" class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline">
            Seat Selection Practice
          </a>
          .
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 sm:text-base">
      <h2 class="section-h2 text-slate-950">Aisle seat or middle seat?</h2>
      <p>This is preference, not a rule &mdash; and it changes with your group.</p>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>Aisle seat:</strong> easy in and out, extra legroom, but foot traffic and people climbing past you all night.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Middle seat:</strong> nobody climbs past you, but you are locked in for the set.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>For groups of 3&ndash;4:</strong> the middle seats are what keep the group together. The aisle is the edge of your block, not the middle of it &mdash; and the simulator caps a drill at 4 seats, so a four-seat block has to fit on one side of an aisle.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 sm:text-base">
      <h2 class="section-h2 text-slate-950">Set the budget before the map opens</h2>
      <p>
        The tiers make the budget decision simple. A $140 per-ticket cap rules out the floor entirely ($185&ndash;$195) and turns the choice into lower bowl vs. upper terrace &mdash; a two-option decision, not a five-option panic. That is the whole trick: decide the cap first, then the map only shows you what you can actually take. The cap technique in full, with the on-screen markers to watch for, is in{' '}
        <a href="/guides/seat-selection-practice/" class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline">
          Seat Selection Practice
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 sm:text-base">
      <h2 class="section-h2 text-slate-950">Which tier, when?</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>You want the pit energy and the budget allows</strong> &mdash; Floor.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You want the best view per dollar</strong> &mdash; Lower Bowl.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You are bringing kids, you want the whole show, or the budget is tight</strong> &mdash; Upper Terrace Center.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You are unsure</strong> &mdash; Lower Bowl. It is the hardest tier to regret.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 sm:text-base">
      <h2 class="section-h2 text-slate-950">Try it yourself</h2>
      <p>
        Run the same drill three times in the{' '}
        <a href="/practice/" class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline">
          Practice Arena
        </a>{' '}
        &mdash; once targeting the floor, once the lower bowl, once the upper terrace &mdash; and compare how each feels. The score breakdown shows you exactly what each choice cost, and the{' '}
        <a href="/guides/scoring-explained/" class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline">
          scoring guide
        </a>{' '}
        explains how every point is earned.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 sm:text-base">
      <h2 class="section-h2 text-slate-950">Honest limits</h2>
      <p>
        Starlight Civic Arena is fictional and its prices are virtual. Real venues differ &mdash; some have pits, some have suites, some put the stage at the end instead of the center &mdash; and real prices move with demand. What transfers is the decision skill: read the tier, pick the trade-off, commit fast. What no simulator can change &mdash; queue placement, live inventory, payment checks &mdash; is covered in{' '}
        <a href="/guides/ticketmaster-training/" class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline">
          Ticketmaster Training: Scope &amp; Limits
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-extrabold text-slate-950">Practice Reading the Arena Map</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a
          href="/practice/"
          class="rounded-xl bg-indigo-600 px-4 py-2.5 text-sm font-bold text-white hover:bg-indigo-500"
        >
          Open Practice Arena &rarr;
        </a>
        <a
          href="/guides/scoring-explained/"
          class="rounded-xl border border-slate-300 bg-slate-50 px-4 py-2.5 text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 hover:bg-slate-100"
        >
          Read Scoring Explained
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
